01:04:15.280 --> 01:04:29.920
Kostadis: When nestled in the rocky foothills of the snow-capped mountains, of the snow-capped Sword Mountains, is the mining town of Phandalin, which consists of 40 or 50 simple log buildings.

937
01:04:30.240 --> 01:04:38.800
Kostadis: Crumbling stone ruins surround the newer houses and shop, showing how this must have been a much larger town in centuries past.

938
01:04:39.360 --> 01:04:46.320
Kostadis: Phandalin's residents are quiet, hard-working folk who came from distant cities to eke out a life amid the harsh wilderness.

939
01:04:46.640 --> 01:04:50.480
Kostadis: They are farmers, stonecutters, blacksmiths, traders, prospectors, and children.

940
01:04:50.800 --> 01:04:52.720
Kostadis: The town has no walls and no garrison.

941
01:04:53.200 --> 01:04:57.920
Kostadis: Most of the adults keep weapons within easy reach in case the need for arms should arise.

942
01:04:58.240 --> 01:05:04.160
Kostadis: Visitors are welcome here, especially if they have coin to spend or news to share.

943
01:05:04.960 --> 01:05:08.400
Kostadis: The Stonehill Inn at the center of the town offers modest meals.

944
01:05:08.560 --> 01:05:11.040
Kostadis: A couple of doors from the inn posted outside.

945
01:05:11.360 --> 01:05:16.800
Kostadis: The Townmaster's Hall is a job for adventurers.

946
01:05:20.630 --> 01:05:24.870
Kostadis: So, I presume you go to the Townmaster's Hall.

947
01:05:26.710 --> 01:05:28.150
Wade: How long have we been walking?

948
01:05:29.110 --> 01:05:31.670
Kostadis: I'm assuming you've been walking for several days.

949
01:05:31.830 --> 01:05:33.510
Kostadis: It's kind of like early in the morning.

950
01:05:33.670 --> 01:05:34.870
Kostadis: Like, it's up to you.

951
01:05:35.190 --> 01:05:38.550
Wade: You know, meals sounds a little better than just running straight to work.

952
01:05:38.870 --> 01:05:39.110
Dave [?]: All right.

953
01:05:39.190 --> 01:05:39.510
Wade [?]: To the inn.

954
01:05:39.670 --> 01:05:40.870
Wade [?]: Are the meals free?

955
01:05:41.430 --> 01:05:41.910
Wade: Huh?

956
01:05:42.230 --> 01:05:43.670
Dave: Are the meals free?

957
01:05:44.630 --> 01:05:47.030
Wade: Where have you been living that meals are free?

958
01:05:47.990 --> 01:05:50.230
Dave: Well, I don't have any money for the meal.

959
01:05:50.390 --> 01:05:52.790
Dave: I'll just eat my rations outside.

960
01:05:54.660 --> 01:05:55.860
Dave: You guys go ahead.

961
01:05:56.500 --> 01:05:58.260
Wade: Starving artists.

962
01:06:04.320 --> 01:06:04.960
Kostadis: All right.

963
01:06:05.519 --> 01:06:12.800
Kostadis: So these are, as you're walking by, you see these stores on the left and the right of the road.

964
01:06:15.760 --> 01:06:20.559
Kostadis: And you, as you, and across the street, you see a Lionshield Coster.

965
01:06:22.079 --> 01:06:28.480
Kostadis: The famous interdimensional store where one used to be able to buy everything.

966
01:06:30.079 --> 01:06:34.480
Kostadis: But apparently, rumor has it that their supply chain has been disrupted.

967
01:06:34.800 --> 01:06:36.159
Wade: Damn COVID.

968
01:06:36.480 --> 01:06:37.280
Wade [?]: Terrifying.

969
01:06:38.079 --> 01:06:38.320
Wade [?]: What?

970
01:06:39.119 --> 01:06:39.840
Dave: Tariffs.

971
01:06:40.400 --> 01:06:41.440
Wade: Oh, tariffs, yeah.

972
01:06:42.239 --> 01:06:44.480
Wade [?]: Sorry, I'm not up to date on my memes.

973
01:06:45.440 --> 01:06:46.079
UNKNOWN: All right.

974
01:06:46.400 --> 01:06:46.719
Kostadis: Okay.

975
01:06:47.360 --> 01:06:49.199
Kostadis: So you want to go to the Stonehill Inn.

976
01:06:49.440 --> 01:06:50.400
Kostadis: Is that what I heard?

977
01:06:50.960 --> 01:06:59.440
Wade: I mean, I feel like after traveling for a day, it's just getting off our feet for a while sounds better than getting a negotiation.

978
01:07:01.679 --> 01:07:04.719
Wade: But if the bard has, you know, his drawers, we won't.

979
01:07:05.039 --> 01:07:07.440
Dave: Maybe I could play for my meal.

980
01:07:08.639 --> 01:07:11.920
Wade: You could sit outside and play and see if you can, you know, upturn your hat or whatever.

981
01:07:12.079 --> 01:07:14.960
Dave: Maybe people drop some coin performance for money.

982
01:07:15.199 --> 01:07:16.079
Dave: Yeah, I would do that.

983
01:07:16.400 --> 01:07:16.719
Kostadis: All right.

984
01:07:16.960 --> 01:07:20.639
Kostadis: So the Stonehill Inn was a modest two-story roadhouse.

985
01:07:22.400 --> 01:07:26.079
Kostadis: It has several rooms for rent on the upper floor.

986
01:07:26.400 --> 01:07:31.280
Kostadis: And the proprietor is a short, friendly male human named Toblin Stonehill.

987
01:07:31.840 --> 01:07:36.079
Kostadis: Toblin is very glad to see you.

988
01:07:37.280 --> 01:07:38.639
Dave [?]: Business is hard for him.

989
01:07:38.800 --> 01:07:39.599
Dave: Does he know it?

990
01:07:40.480 --> 01:07:42.239
Kostadis: No, he's very glad to see you.

991
01:07:42.320 --> 01:07:43.199
Kostadis: Your business.

992
01:07:43.519 --> 01:07:43.760
Kostadis [?]: Yeah.

993
01:07:44.559 --> 01:07:44.800
Kostadis: Right?

994
01:07:47.920 --> 01:07:53.679
Kostadis: He says hello to you, walks up to a table, starts cleaning it, and says, please sit.

995
01:07:53.920 --> 01:07:55.519
Kostadis: Rest your weary legs.

996
01:07:55.760 --> 01:07:56.960
Kostadis: How can I help you?

997
01:07:57.280 --> 01:07:58.000
Wade: Absolutely.

998
01:07:58.239 --> 01:07:58.880
Wade: Tea.

999
01:08:00.960 --> 01:08:01.599
Wade [?]: Tea?

1000
01:08:03.440 --> 01:08:03.840
Kostadis: Tea?

1001
01:08:04.079 --> 01:08:04.960
Kostadis: Just tea?

1002
01:08:05.280 --> 01:08:06.400
Kostadis [?]: Just tea.

1003
01:08:06.719 --> 01:08:09.280
Kostadis: You want me to boil water and give it to you?

1004
01:08:09.599 --> 01:08:10.159
Kostadis [?]: Yes.

1005
01:08:11.760 --> 01:08:14.079
Wade: I have the herbs myself if you don't.

1006
01:08:15.679 --> 01:08:20.239
Kostadis: He looks at you, and you're somewhat of a large creature, and he looks at his chair.

1007
01:08:22.319 --> 01:08:24.800
Wade: Oh, we're getting to the fat shaving now.

1008
01:08:26.720 --> 01:08:31.439
Kostadis: He says, I think you should sit right here, but if you don't mind, I'd like to get the sturdier chair.

1009
01:08:31.760 --> 01:08:32.239
Kostadis: He comes back.

1010
01:08:33.840 --> 01:08:36.079
Kostadis: He comes back with a sturdier chair.

1011
01:08:37.600 --> 01:08:38.159
UNKNOWN: All right.

1012
01:08:38.479 --> 01:08:44.640
UNKNOWN: He says, well, that will be 10 copper pieces for the boiled water.

1013
01:08:45.600 --> 01:08:47.119
Kostadis [?]: I assume I have some amount of starting.

1014
01:08:47.279 --> 01:08:50.159
Dave: I want to play for your inn for a while.

1015
01:08:50.479 --> 01:08:51.359
Dave: I have no money.

1016
01:08:53.860 --> 01:08:56.020
Dave: I got a 17 on my performance roll.

1017
01:08:56.260 --> 01:08:57.460
UNKNOWN: I'm very good, actually.

1018
01:08:59.940 --> 01:09:10.820
Kostadis: He pauses and says, well, if you have something, one of the more fashionable tunes from Waterdeep or from Neverwinter, I think, or even Tribor.

1019
01:09:11.300 --> 01:09:13.220
Dave: Yes, yes, one of those.

1020
01:09:13.539 --> 01:09:15.140
Kostadis: Yeah, I'd be delighted to hear that.

1021
01:09:15.300 --> 01:09:20.340
Kostadis [?]: Of course, that's what those bangers here play Freebird.

1022
01:09:20.659 --> 01:09:21.619
Dave: Yeah, totally.

1023
01:09:21.940 --> 01:09:22.659
Dave: Freebird.

1024
01:09:23.940 --> 01:09:24.900
Kostadis: He seems upset.

1025
01:09:25.380 --> 01:09:26.340
Dave [?]: No, no, no, no, no, no.

1026
01:09:26.420 --> 01:09:27.539
UNKNOWN: I'll play whatever you want.

1027
01:09:27.779 --> 01:09:28.820
UNKNOWN: Whatever you want.

1028
01:09:30.340 --> 01:09:32.340
Kostadis: He goes, Toblin is a little bit upset.

1029
01:09:32.420 --> 01:09:34.260
Kostadis: He goes, I didn't realize that.

1030
01:09:35.300 --> 01:09:37.140
Kostadis: My music, if you have some.

1031
01:09:37.940 --> 01:09:39.140
Dave: I'm here to please.

1032
01:09:39.460 --> 01:09:40.820
Kostadis: I'm desperate.

1033
01:09:41.779 --> 01:09:49.699
Kostadis: He goes, well, I mean, if you're willing to have one of my creations, I think I could be even entertained by one of yours.

1034
01:09:50.980 --> 01:09:52.180
Kostadis [?]: What are your creations?

1035
01:09:53.300 --> 01:09:55.779
Kostadis: You seem to want to play a music piece of your own.

1036
01:09:57.779 --> 01:09:58.260
Dave: Well, okay.

1037
01:09:58.420 --> 01:10:00.579
Dave: So I can spend the evening playing music.

1038
01:10:00.659 --> 01:10:03.539
Dave: And I don't know if he has a piano, but I have a couple instruments.

1039
01:10:04.100 --> 01:10:04.340
Kostadis: All right.

1040
01:10:04.420 --> 01:10:04.579
Kostadis: Okay.

1041
01:10:04.659 --> 01:10:06.340
Kostadis: Roll your performance to see how well he is.

1042
01:10:06.579 --> 01:10:07.140
Dave: I got a 17.

1043
01:10:08.340 --> 01:10:09.940
Kostadis: He is very impressed with your music.

1044
01:10:10.100 --> 01:10:11.060
Kostadis: He's very delighted.

1045
01:10:12.739 --> 01:10:18.020
Kostadis: As you're playing the music, he goes, Well, that is definitely worth some ale.

1046
01:10:18.180 --> 01:10:21.060
Kostadis: And he pours you a glass of beer and hands it to you.

1047
01:10:21.380 --> 01:10:22.820
Dave: But did you love it?

1048
01:10:25.699 --> 01:10:28.180
Kostadis: He smiles and roll your perception.

1049
01:10:28.260 --> 01:10:29.619
Kostadis: What's your perception?

1050
01:10:33.619 --> 01:10:37.300
Dave: I got a 15 on my perception roll.

1051
01:10:37.619 --> 01:10:42.739
Kostadis: All right, now roll your insight or investigation, whichever one's higher.

1052
01:10:43.460 --> 01:10:43.860
Dave: Four.

1053
01:10:44.820 --> 01:10:48.420
Kostadis: Okay, you definitely are not sure whether he's telling the truth.

1054
01:10:48.739 --> 01:10:53.060
Dave: Well, if you didn't love it, I can't take your beer.

1055
01:10:53.619 --> 01:10:55.779
Gary: With a four, you probably believe him.

1056
01:10:56.500 --> 01:10:57.380
Gary [?]: Yeah, actually, no, that's true.

1057
01:10:57.460 --> 01:10:58.340
UNKNOWN: With a four, you believe him.

1058
01:10:58.739 --> 01:10:59.300
Dave: I believe it.

1059
01:10:59.539 --> 01:10:59.699
Dave: All right.

1060
01:10:59.940 --> 01:11:00.420
Kostadis [?]: Well, then I believe it.

1061
01:11:00.500 --> 01:11:02.820
Kostadis: Well, yeah, you did not perceive the lie, right?

1062
01:11:03.060 --> 01:11:03.460
Kostadis: Right, yeah.

1063
01:11:03.619 --> 01:11:04.100
Kostadis: So, yes.

1064
01:11:05.380 --> 01:11:06.579
Kostadis: No, let me try this differently.

1065
01:11:06.900 --> 01:11:09.220
Kostadis: You got the impression that he adored your music.

1066
01:11:09.460 --> 01:11:09.699
Dave: All right.

1067
01:11:09.779 --> 01:11:10.980
Dave: Well, then I will take the beer.

1068
01:11:11.140 --> 01:11:11.300
Dave [?]: Right.

1069
01:11:11.860 --> 01:11:13.699
Kostadis [?]: Sometimes it's good to be an idiot.

1070
01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:16.739
Kostadis: Bramgrim.

1071
01:11:16.980 --> 01:11:17.539
Kostadis: Here's his bless.

1072
01:11:18.260 --> 01:11:18.420
Kostadis [?]: All right.

1073
01:11:18.500 --> 01:11:20.100
Dave: So Bramgrim liked it.

1074
01:11:20.739 --> 01:11:22.020
Dave: He's my number one fan.

1075
01:11:22.900 --> 01:11:23.060
Kostadis [?]: Yeah.

1076
01:11:25.869 --> 01:11:26.349
Kostadis: All right.

1077
01:11:26.510 --> 01:11:29.389
Kostadis: So he apparently I'm supposed to reveal a story here.

1078
01:11:29.710 --> 01:11:30.510
Kostadis [?]: Excellent.

1079
01:11:33.399 --> 01:11:38.119
Gary: And I think Felkur was bored with all this display of music.

1080
01:11:38.439 --> 01:11:39.000
Gary [?]: All right.

1081
01:11:41.390 --> 01:11:43.310
Gary: We'll say she's not a music lover.

1082
01:11:46.510 --> 01:11:51.710
Kostadis: She goes, once again, the orcs have come down from the mountains to prey on the lowlands.

1083
01:11:52.029 --> 01:11:59.470
Kostadis: If Neverwinter doesn't send help soon, the orcs will overrun Phandalin and destroy everything we work so hard to rebuild.

1084
01:12:00.110 --> 01:12:00.989
Kostadis: Pauses.

1085
01:12:01.310 --> 01:12:03.150
Kostadis: Do you have anything to do with Phandalin?

1086
01:12:03.310 --> 01:12:07.310
Kostadis: Like, are you part of the support Neverwinter is sending to us?

1087
01:12:07.949 --> 01:12:08.510
Kostadis [?]: Yeah, that's right.

1088
01:12:08.829 --> 01:12:10.110
Dave [?]: If there's money in it.

1089
01:12:11.949 --> 01:12:13.230
Kostadis: Well, I.

1090
01:12:13.869 --> 01:12:14.909
Kostadis: Ah, money?

1091
01:12:18.279 --> 01:12:21.799
Kostadis: I thought Neverwinter was going to pay you, not us.

1092
01:12:23.000 --> 01:12:26.599
Kostadis: Oh, your adventures.

1093
01:12:28.119 --> 01:12:29.159
Kostadis [?]: What gave it away?

1094
01:12:29.399 --> 01:12:31.559
Dave: I don't really understand how we're going to get paid.

1095
01:12:31.879 --> 01:12:33.479
Kostadis: Well, that's very easy, he says.

1096
01:12:33.719 --> 01:12:36.439
Kostadis: Outside the Townmaster's Hall, there is a quest board.

1097
01:12:37.079 --> 01:12:43.399
Kostadis: The Townmaster, the city has some problems, and it's best if you go there and check them out.

1098
01:12:44.279 --> 01:12:44.519
Dave: Okay.

1099
01:12:46.279 --> 01:12:48.920
Kostadis [?]: Are you dealing with these orcs on that list?

1100
01:12:51.159 --> 01:12:56.199
Kostadis: Well, you'd have to go check what the townmaster has decided is important enough for the city to collect.

1101
01:12:56.519 --> 01:13:00.199
Kostadis: He is, in my mind, I'm not sure how he prioritizes things.

1102
01:13:00.759 --> 01:13:05.319
Wade: Do you know why the orcs are causing trouble lately?

1103
01:13:05.960 --> 01:13:16.039
Kostadis: Well, you must have heard of the War of the Dragons and the War of the Giants, followed by the barbarian hordes, followed by the disturbances in the Underdark.

1104
01:13:16.359 --> 01:13:19.159
Wade: Oh, the world is always at war.

1105
01:13:19.399 --> 01:13:21.879
Wade: What's special about these orcs today?

1106
01:13:22.519 --> 01:13:34.679
Kostadis: Well, usually during these orcs like to expand, and when there isn't a strong army to hold them back, they expand into territories that don't belong to them.

1107
01:13:35.000 --> 01:13:39.000
Wade: So, wait, was there a strong army here previously that disbanded?

1108
01:13:39.639 --> 01:13:44.279
Kostadis: Well, the various wars depleted Neverwinter's resources.

1109
01:13:45.879 --> 01:13:46.359
Wade [?]: And so.

1110
01:13:47.000 --> 01:13:51.319
Kostadis [?]: This place doesn't look like it ever held a lot of troops in the past, I don't know, 200 years.

1111
01:13:51.639 --> 01:13:52.199
Kostadis: No, no, no.

1112
01:13:53.399 --> 01:13:56.199
Kostadis: Phandalin has never been a place with a large army.

1113
01:13:56.359 --> 01:14:00.599
Kostadis: It was overrun by orcs many years ago, but since then it's recovered.

1114
01:14:00.759 --> 01:14:08.279
Kostadis: I, in fact, lived in Tribor and managed to get word about the disaster that came there from the Uthgaritian barbarians.

1115
01:14:08.599 --> 01:14:09.399
Kostadis: He looks at you.

1116
01:14:09.639 --> 01:14:12.359
Kostadis: You wouldn't be one of those barbarians, would you?

1117
01:14:12.679 --> 01:14:14.359
Kostadis: I'm so sorry.

1118
01:14:17.799 --> 01:14:19.960
Wade: Are you apologizing for your ancestors?

1119
01:14:20.279 --> 01:14:21.399
Dave: I can vouch for him.

1120
01:14:21.559 --> 01:14:23.000
Dave: He really loves music.

1121
01:14:23.319 --> 01:14:23.639
Wade [?]: All right.

1122
01:14:23.879 --> 01:14:24.199
Wade [?]: He goes.

1123
01:14:25.079 --> 01:14:27.479
Dave: He's not like the other barbarian.

1124
01:14:27.799 --> 01:14:28.759
Wade: I've known him a week.

1125
01:14:28.920 --> 01:14:29.960
Wade: He seems fine.

1126
01:14:30.279 --> 01:14:30.439
Wade [?]: Right.

1127
01:14:31.079 --> 01:14:31.559
Wade [?]: He paused.

1128
01:14:32.439 --> 01:14:32.920
Kostadis: What's his name?

1129
01:14:33.079 --> 01:14:35.639
Kostadis: Pauses and goes, look, as far.

1130
01:14:36.199 --> 01:14:46.920
Kostadis: Toblin pauses and goes, look, my friends, I think that whatever plans you might have and whatever adventures you might have and whatever stories you might have, all is forgiven between us.

1131
01:14:47.319 --> 01:14:48.519
Kostadis: The past is the past.

1132
01:14:48.759 --> 01:14:50.519
Kostadis: The future is the future.

1133
01:14:50.839 --> 01:14:52.359
Dave: What are you forgiving me for?

1134
01:14:52.679 --> 01:14:53.479
Kostadis: I'm not forgiving you.

1135
01:14:53.559 --> 01:14:54.759
Kostadis: I'm forgiving all barbarians.

1136
01:14:55.239 --> 01:14:55.559
Kostadis: I'm telling.

1137
01:14:55.719 --> 01:14:56.039
Kostadis: I'm talking about.

1138
01:14:56.679 --> 01:14:57.799
Dave: The barbarian.

1139
01:14:58.119 --> 01:14:58.439
Wade [?]: Yes.

1140
01:14:58.759 --> 01:14:59.159
Dave: Oh, yeah.

1141
01:14:59.399 --> 01:15:01.639
Wade: He's following a hashtag not all barbarians.

1142
01:15:02.039 --> 01:15:03.159
Dave [?]: Yes, not all barbarians.

1143
01:15:08.359 --> 01:15:09.159
Dave: Well said.

1144
01:15:09.479 --> 01:15:09.719
Kostadis: Right.

1145
01:15:09.960 --> 01:15:10.199
Kostadis: Okay.

1146
01:15:10.359 --> 01:15:15.319
Kostadis: And so he's like, but I do recommend you go talk to you if you're looking for work.

1147
01:15:15.960 --> 01:15:19.319
Kostadis: Well, if you're looking to spend some money, I don't need a place to stay.

1148
01:15:19.479 --> 01:15:20.759
Kostadis: I do have a room.

1149
01:15:21.719 --> 01:15:22.359
Gary: Bramgrim.

1150
01:15:22.519 --> 01:15:23.719
Gary: I can't afford to live here.

1151
01:15:23.879 --> 01:15:26.279
Wade: Yeah, I don't think we've got enough money for all of us.

1152
01:15:27.479 --> 01:15:29.239
Kostadis [?]: We'll have to go collect some coin.

1153
01:15:29.559 --> 01:15:33.399
Kostadis: Yes, well, in that case, I do recommend you go check the quest board out.

1154
01:15:34.279 --> 01:15:34.679
Kostadis [?]: Great.

1155
01:15:35.559 --> 01:15:36.199
Wade: All right.

1156
01:15:37.399 --> 01:15:39.159
